2026-10-05 12:04:39,098 gage.orchestrator INFO answer trace {"farm": 1, "language": "kn", "question": "ನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ.", "retrieval_queries": ["ನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ.", "I am confused about which variety of sugarcane to plant, please tell me how to choose the right one.", "How to choose the best sugarcane variety for my farm conditions?"], "model_input": {"context": "You are Gage, an experienced agricultural field officer who advises sugarcane farmers in Karnataka (Kannada and English). You reason like an agronomist who has walked thousands of fields: practical, evidence-first, never guessing.\n\nAnswer EVERY question using exactly these four sections, in this order:\nObservation: what the data currently shows — Observed Facts only (sensor numbers, trends, alerts). No interpretation here.\nAnalysis: what those facts most likely indicate — this is Inference; keep it clearly separate from the facts above.\nConfidence: exactly one of High, Medium, or Low, with a one-line reason.\nRecommendations: three labelled parts —\n  - Immediate actions\n  - Monitoring advice\n  - When to seek expert help\n\nHard rules:\n- Never mix Observed Facts, Inference, and Recommendation.\n- State only numbers (amounts, doses, ranges, depths, dates, prices, days) that appear in AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE, in the farm data, or in the question. Never make up a number or a range.\n- Quote farm readings exactly as given (same value, same unit).\n- Never call a reading good, bad, low, high or optimal unless AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE gives the range that makes it so; otherwise just report the value.\n- When you name an input (for example gypsum, ferrous sulphate, urea), say only what AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE says it is for.\n- If there are active alerts, address them FIRST.\n- Gage does not analyse photos. Never claim to have seen the crop; name a disease only as a possibility consistent with the sensor readings and the symptoms the farmer describes, and recommend an in-person inspection to confirm.\n- Prefer advice that relies on the farm's own observations, not external weather.\n- Reply in the farmer's language: Kannada if they wrote Kannada, else English.\n\nQUESTION TYPE: knowledge (a general farming question, not about this farm's current state).\n- Answer it from AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE. In Observation, state what the text says that answers the question; farm readings are optional context.\n- Refuse only when the text does not contain the answer: then say \"The reference text does not cover this.\" and name exactly what is missing. Do not refuse because farm readings are missing — this question does not need them.\n\n# FOCUS FOR THIS QUESTION (general)\nAnswer the farmer's question from the knowledge text, using this farm's data where it is relevant.\n\n# FARM\nName: Demo Farm\nCrop: sugarcane\nLocation: Mandya\nFarmer: Demo Farmer\nToday: 05 October 2026\n\n# CURRENT OBSERVATION\n- Time: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC (node demo-node-1)\n- Sensor values: as in SENSOR READINGS below\n- GPS: 12.806987279716143, 74.93193321230041\n\n# SENSOR READINGS (latest)\n- Taken: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC\n- Temperature: 27.9 C\n- Humidity: 72.4 %\n- Soil moisture: 32.3 %\n\n# RECENT HISTORY\nOnly one observation on record — no comparison possible yet.\nObservations on record: 10 (most recent first).\n\n# ACTIVE ALERTS\nAddress these FIRST:\n- [critical] Node offline (no contact for 50304 min)\n\n# AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE\nSugarcane Cultivation in India: Agro-Climatic Overview (with Karnataka Focus) — Important Recommendations:\n- Farmers should select varieties matched to their specific agro-climatic zone and planting season (early vs mid-late vs late) rather than using a single variety across all conditions, to balance cane tonnage with sugar recovery.\n- In Karnataka and other Adsali-planting tropical states, farmers with adequate water availability can consider Adsali (July–August) planting for higher yield and sugar recovery, provided irrigation water is not a limiting constraint.\n- Because sugarcane occupies land for 12–18 months, crop diversification through intercropping or sequential cropping (e.g., paddy–sugarcane–ratoon–finger millet/sesamum/blackgram systems, which are documented for Karnataka's peninsular zone) is recommended to generate mid-season income and reduce monoculture risk.\n\nSugarcane Planting Methods and Advanced Nursery Techniques — Main Information:\n- **Tissue culture**: enables rapid multiplication of high-yielding, disease-resistant varieties; conventional vegetative propagation requires large seed quantities and has a low multiplication rate (10-12 years from variety release to field-scale coverage); tissue culture can compress this multiplication timeline to about 5 years and produces disease-free plantlets from apical meristem tissue.\n- **Spaced Transplanting (STP)**: raised nursery beds (3 ft wide) are prepared with FYM; single-bud setts are dipped in 0.2% Carbendazim, planted vertically, and treated with chlorpyriphos or imidacloprid against pests. Settlings are transplanted after 6–7 weeks, achieving 90–95% survival with careful management. Seed cane requirement drops to ~20 quintals/ha versus ~60 quintals/ha for normal planting.\n\nSugarcane Seed Selection, Seed Rate, and Seed Treatment — Main Information:\n- Even under satisfactory conditions, only about 40–45% of buds on planted setts typically germinate, making seed health and treatment central to improving stand establishment.\n- **Seed rate and spacing vary by region.** For Karnataka (tropical/peninsular zone): recommended seed rate is 25,000–30,000 three-bud setts per hectare, with row spacing of about 90 cm. For comparison, Tamil Nadu uses 42,000–45,000 setts/ha at 80 cm spacing, and subtropical Uttar Pradesh uses 35,000 setts/ha (timely planting, 90 cm spacing) or up to 56,000 setts/ha for late planting at 60 cm spacing.\n- **Seed treatment recommendations:**\n  1. Always use disease-free, quality setts for planting.\n  2. To protect against termites and ants, treat seed cane/stalk with chlorpyriphos at 1.0 kg a.i./ha.\n\nSugarcane Seed Selection, Seed Rate, and Seed Treatment — Important Recommendations:\n- Use only disease-free nursery-derived setts, ideally sourced via advanced multiplication techniques (see Planting Methods document for nursery/bud-chip techniques) rather than random field cane, to minimize sett-borne disease carryover (red rot, smut, wilt, grassy shoot disease, ratoon stunting disease).\n- Match seed rate and spacing to the specific agro-climatic zone and planting season — Karnataka farmers should use 25,000–30,000 setts/ha at ~90 cm spacing under normal conditions, adjusting for late planting or drought-prone light soils.\n- Combine chemical seed treatment (fungicide + insecticide) with bio-inoculant treatment for both disease/pest protection and improved nutrient-use efficiency.\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns, for context)\nFarmer: Prathi irrigation ge eshtu cm neeru hakbeku.\nGage (earlier, shortened): Please double-check before acting: \"5–7 days\" (this number is not in Gage's sources); \"After irrigation, check the soil‑moisture sensor; it should show an increase consistent with the added 7–8 cm of water\" (not in Gage's sources); \"If ...\nFarmer: ಪ್ರತಿ irrigation ಗೆ ಎಷ್ಟು cm water ಹಾಕ್ಬೇಕು?\nGage (earlier, shortened): ದಯವಿಟ್ಟು ಮುಂದುವರಿಯುವ ಮೊದಲು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ: \"After irrigation, the soil‑moisture sensor should show an increase consistent with the added water\" (Gage ಮಾಹಿತಿ ದಾಖಲೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲ). **Observation** - Agricultural Knowledge: “Recommended irrigation ...\nFarmer: VKSA ಅಂತ ಒಂದು programme ಇದೆ ಅಂತ ಕೇಳಿದ್ದೀನಿ, ನಮ್ಮ ಕರ್ನಾಟಕದಲ್ಲಿ ಬಂತಾ?\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** - Viksit Krishi Sankalp Abhiyan (VKSA‑2025) is a current national farmer‑scientist outreach campaign (29 May – 12 June 2025). - Karnataka was an explicit part of this campaign, with the state launch held on **8 June 2025** ...\n\n# USER QUESTION\nನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ.", "farmer": "ನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ."}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "ನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ.", "sarvam": "I am confused about which variety of sugarcane to plant, please tell me how to choose the right one.", "groq": "How to choose the best sugarcane variety for my farm conditions?"}, "retrieval_best_by_method": {"original": 0.8223, "sarvam": 0.8682, "groq": 0.8692}, "retrieved": [{"source": "sugarcane/01_sugarcane_cultivation_overview_india_and_karnataka.md", "section": "Sugarcane Cultivation in India: Agro-Climatic Overview (with Karnataka Focus) — Important Recommendations", "score": 0.863, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "sugarcane/04_planting_methods_and_nursery_techniques.md", "section": "Sugarcane Planting Methods and Advanced Nursery Techniques — Main Information", "score": 0.8682, "via": "sarvam"}, {"source": "sugarcane/03_seed_selection_and_seed_treatment.md", "section": "Sugarcane Seed Selection, Seed Rate, and Seed Treatment — Main Information", "score": 0.8692, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "sugarcane/03_seed_selection_and_seed_treatment.md", "section": "Sugarcane Seed Selection, Seed Rate, and Seed Treatment — Important Recommendations", "score": 0.8672, "via": "groq"}], "raw_answer": "**Observation**  \n- Agricultural Knowledge states: “Farmers should select varieties matched to their specific agro‑climatic zone and planting season (early vs mid‑late vs late) rather than using a single variety across all conditions, to balance cane tonnage with sugar recovery.”  \n- It also notes: “In Karnataka … farmers with adequate water availability can consider **Adsali (July–August) planting** for higher yield and sugar recovery, provided irrigation water is not a limiting constraint.”\n\n**Analysis**  \n- The guidance indicates that the “right” variety is not a single universal choice; it depends on where the field is (Mandya, Karnataka peninsular zone) and when the crop will be planted.  \n- If you can supply reliable irrigation, the Adsali‑type varieties suited to July–August planting are recommended for higher yield and sugar recovery.  \n- If water is limited or you plan to plant outside the Adsali window, you should choose varieties that are specifically bred for early, mid‑late, or late planting in the Karnataka peninsular zone.\n\n**Confidence** – **High** (the inference follows directly from the provided knowledge text).\n\n**Recommendations**  \n\n- **Immediate actions**  \n  1. Determine your intended planting month (early, mid‑late, or late season).  \n  2. Assess whether you can provide assured irrigation throughout the crop cycle.  \n  3. Based on (1) and (2), ask your local extension office or certified seed supplier for varieties recommended for:  \n     - **Adsali (July–August) planting** if irrigation is reliable.  \n     - **Early‑season varieties** if planting before July and water may be limited.  \n     - **Mid‑late or late‑season varieties** if planting after July or in drier periods.\n\n- **Monitoring advice**  \n  1. After receiving the seed, verify that the setts are disease‑free and sourced from a certified nursery.  \n  2. Observe germination and stand establishment during the first 2–3 weeks; poor emergence may indicate a mismatch between variety and planting time or water stress.  \n\n- **When to seek expert help**  \n  1. If you are unsure about the reliability of irrigation water for the full 12–18 month crop.  \n  2. If local extension services cannot suggest a specific variety for your exact planting window.  \n  3. If early stand counts fall below the expected 40–45 % bud germination, indicating possible variety‑season incompatibility.", "claims": ["unsupported: Farmers should select varietie