e Maturity Indicators and Harvesting Practices — Main Information:\n- **Cane yield**: a good crop in north India yields about 80–100 tonnes/ha; in south India (particularly Adsali crop), yield can exceed 150 tonnes/ha.\n- **Harvesting for good ratoon establishment**: good ratoon crops come from good plant crops harvested under favourable conditions. Autumn-planted cane harvested early in the crushing season gives better sprouting and ratoon yield. Harvesting should be done close to the ground level to promote better sprouting and survival of underground buds/tillers.\n- Prior to harvest, **tying and wrapping** operations (typically done in August, when cane reaches about 2 m height) provide mechanical support against lodging: leaves are removed and canes bundled together, then clumps in adjacent rows are cross-tied. Green leaves should not be bundled together while tying.\n\nIntegrated Insect-Pest Management in Sugarcane — Main Information:\n- **Early shoot and root borer**: Application of Chlorpyriphos 20 EC at 5 litres/ha in 1,600–1,800 litres water (3 ml/litre) on cane setts in furrows at planting. Collection and destruction of infested shoots on a campaign basis from March to May is also recommended.\n- **Termite**: Chlorpyriphos 20 EC at 5 litres/ha in 1,600–1,800 litres water applied over setts in furrows at planting.\n- **Whitefly**: Removal of lower leaves followed by foliar application of imidacloprid (0.005%) + 2% urea; urea solution should be prepared first, then imidacloprid added, to avoid coagulation.\n- **Mealy bug**: Sett treatment with imidacloprid 70 WG/SP or thiamethoxam 70 WG/SP (25 g a.i./ha), followed by foliar spraying of imidacloprid 17.8 SL (0.05%) at cane formation stage.\n- **Rodents**: Zinc phosphide (2%) baiting followed by bromadiolone baiting.\n\nSugarcane Disease Management — Important Recommendations:\n- Because most of these diseases (red rot, smut, wilt, grassy shoot, ratoon stunting, yellow leaf) are seed/sett-transmitted, the single highest-leverage preventive action for farmers is sourcing certified disease-free planting material (ideally tissue-culture or nursery-derived, per the Planting Methods document) rather than relying on curative treatment after symptoms appear.\n- Hot water treatment at 50–52°C for 2–3 hours is a recurring, low-cost control measure across multiple diseases (smut, grassy shoot, ratoon stunting) and should be a standard nursery practice before establishing new seed material.\n- For fungal foliar diseases (pokkah boeng, rust), timely fungicide spraying at first symptom appearance, repeated at 15-day intervals, is more effective than a single late-stage application.\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns, for context)\nFarmer: VKSA ಅಂತ ಒಂದು programme ಇದೆ ಅಂತ ಕೇಳಿದ್ದೀನಿ, ನಮ್ಮ ಕರ್ನಾಟಕದಲ್ಲಿ ಬಂತಾ?\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** - Viksit Krishi Sankalp Abhiyan (VKSA‑2025) is a current national farmer‑scientist outreach campaign (29 May – 12 June 2025). - Karnataka was an explicit part of this campaign, with the state launch held on **8 June 2025** ...\nFarmer: ನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ.\nGage (earlier, shortened): ದಯವಿಟ್ಟು ಮುಂದುವರಿಯುವ ಮೊದಲು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ: \"2–3 weeks\" (ಈ ಸಂಖ್ಯೆ Gage ಮಾಹಿತಿ ದಾಖಲೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲ); \"Farmers should select varieties matched to their specific agro‑climatic zone and planting season rather than using a single variety across all ...\nFarmer: Sir every time watering how much depth I give, any standard number?\nGage (earlier, shortened): Please double-check before acting: \"the recommended depth applies regardless of growth stage\" (contradicts Gage's sources); \"observe the soil‑moisture sensor for an increase consistent with the added water\" (not in Gage's sources); \"adjust ...\n\n# USER QUESTION\nWhy are the leaves of my cane turning yellow?", "farmer": "Why are the leaves of my cane turning yellow?"}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "Why are the leaves of my cane turning y