igation**: ensure the field receives adequate water to raise soil moisture from the current 32.3 % to a level that keeps the root zone moist (avoid prolonged dry periods).  \n3. **Apply a balanced nitrogen fertilizer** (if a soil test later shows N deficiency) – follow local extension rates; do not exceed recommended amounts.  \n\n*Monitoring advice*  \n- **Restore node connectivity** as soon as possible to resume real‑time soil‑moisture and temperature monitoring.  \n- Record the progression of leaf yellowing (percentage of plants affected, severity) and any new symptoms weekly.  \n- If possible, conduct a quick leaf tissue test for nitrogen and other macro‑nutrients.  \n\n*When to seek expert help*  \n- If yellowing spreads rapidly to >30 % of the stand or is accompanied by stunted growth, leaf curl, or pustules.  \n- If the node remains offline for more than 48 hours, preventing you from tracking moisture and temperature trends.  \n- If you suspect **Yellow Leaf Disease** (viral) because the yellowing is uniform and persistent, contact a local agronomist or the district agricultural office for certified disease‑free planting material and possible hot‑water treatment of setts.", "claims": ["supported: Sensor reading: Temperature 27.9 °C", "supported: Sensor reading: Humidity 72.4 %", "supported: Sensor reading: Soil moisture 32.3 %", "supported: Node offline for 50,304 minutes", "unsupported: Leaves turning yellow is a symptom of Yellow Leaf Disease (viral)", "unsupported: Yellowing could indicate nutrient deficiency (e.g., nitrogen)", "unsupported: Yellowing could indicate water stress due to soil moisture 32.3 %", "unsupported: Inspect canopy for stunted shoots, rust pustules, or fungal lesions", "unsupported: Check irrigation to raise soil moisture above current level", "unsupported: Apply balanced nitrogen fertilizer if soil test shows N deficiency", "unsupported: Restore node connectivity to resume real‑time monitoring", "unsupported: Record progression of le