the farmer's language: Kannada if they wrote Kannada, else English.\n\nQUESTION TYPE: farm (about this farm now).\n- Use the farm's sensor readings, alerts and history as the Observed Facts, and AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE for what they mean.\n- If the readings are missing, old or not enough to answer, still say what they do show, then name exactly what is missing (for example a soil-moisture reading, the crop stage, a description of the symptom). Use Confidence Low in that case. Do not refuse outright.\n\n# FOCUS FOR THIS QUESTION (irrigation)\nJudge irrigation timing from the soil-moisture level and its trend and the crop stage. Say whether to irrigate now, wait, or hold, and why.\n\n# FARM\nName: Demo Farm\nCrop: sugarcane\nLocation: Mandya\nFarmer: Demo Farmer\nToday: 05 October 2026\n\n# CURRENT OBSERVATION\n- Time: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC (node demo-node-1)\n- Sensor values: as in SENSOR READINGS below\n- GPS: 12.806987279716143, 74.93193321230041\n\n# SENSOR READINGS (latest)\n- Taken: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC\n- Temperature: 27.9 C\n- Humidity: 72.4 %\n- Soil moisture: 32.3 %\n\n# RECENT HISTORY\nOnly one observation on record — no comparison possible yet.\nObservations on record: 10 (most recent first).\n\n# ACTIVE ALERTS\nAddress these FIRST:\n- [critical] Node offline (no contact for 50304 min)\n\n# AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Main Information:\n- Under drought conditions, contingency guidance also recommends a foliar spray of 1% FeSO₄ + 0.5% ZnSO₄ + 2% urea when iron deficiency is expected due to prolonged dry spells (6–8 weeks delayed monsoon).\n\nSugarcane Cultivation in India: Agro-Climatic Overview (with Karnataka Focus) — Main Information:\n- Sugarcane requires a mean temperature of 28–32°C for optimal growth; temperatures above 45°C reduce tillering, and below 20°C slow growth. Areas with minimum temperature under 5°C are unsuitable.\n- The crop needs 75–120 cm of annual rainfall (if unirrigated) and a long growing season of 10–18 months. Ideal relative humidity is 70–85% during growth and 55–75% during ripening; below 50% humidity during growth is unsuitable.\n- India has two broad sugarcane regions:\n  - **Tropical region** (Maharashtra, Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, Gujarat, Madhya Pradesh, Kerala): ~45% of area, 55% of national production; productivity ~80 t/ha.\n\nPost-Harvest Management of Sugarcane: Preventing Sucrose Loss — Key Facts:\n- Sucrose loss window: deterioration accelerates markedly after 48 hours; staling for 96 hours can cause 7.4–17.0% cane weight loss and ~2.0% sugar recovery loss.\n- Average Indian mill-level loss: 10–15 kg sugar per tonne of cane crushed, due to time lag alone.\n- Moisture loss rate: 1.5–2% per 24 hours after cutting.\n- Best-practice window for processing: within 24–48 hours of cutting, per multiple cross-referenced sources.\n\nSugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Important Recommendations:\n- Given Karnataka's high per-season irrigation requirement (32 irrigations, 266 kg water per kg sugar produced), farmers in the state should prioritize migration from furrow irrigation to drip irrigation, which the source data shows can lower net water requirement from ~1,878 mm/year to ~1,186 mm/year.\n- During monsoon disruptions, adopt the contingency schedule appropriate to the delay: for a 2-week delayed monsoon onset, continue pre-monsoon irrigation intervals (8–10 days) with alternate-furrow application and trash mulching in ratoon; for longer delays (6–8 weeks), shift to a 10-day protective irrigation interval and watch for iron deficiency, which becomes likely under prolonged dry spells (foliar spray of 1% FeSO₄ + 0.5% ZnSO₄ + 2% urea is recommended in that case).\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns, for context)\nFarmer: ನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ.\nGage (earlier, shortened): ದಯವಿಟ್ಟು ಮುಂದುವರಿಯುವ ಮೊದಲು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ: \"2–3 weeks\" (ಈ 