cal fertilizer replenishment (matched to actual nutrient removal, see Fertilizer document) with organic inputs (FYM, press mud, biofertilizers) rather than relying on chemical fertilizer alone, both for cost reduction and for longer-term soil structure/fertility maintenance.\n- For farmers seeking to reduce input costs while sustaining yield, the Sustainable Sugarcane Initiative (SSI) principles — particularly wide spacing and single-bud nursery transplanting — offer a documented alternative production system worth considering, especially where labour and seed cane costs are constraints.\n\nSoil Health Management for Sugarcane — Main Information:\n- The **Sustainable Sugarcane Initiative (SSI)**, developed under an ICRISAT-WWF project, is a documented method combining reduced seed use, reduced water use, and optimized fertilizer/land use. Its core principles include: raising nursery from single-budded chips; transplanting young seedlings (25–35 days old); maintaining wide spacing (5×2 feet) in the main field; providing sufficient moisture while avoiding water inundation; encouraging organic nutrient and plant-protection methods; and intercropping for effective land utilization.\n\n### Soil Health Card Scheme (government program)\n- The Soil Health Card Scheme provides for periodic testing of soil and nutrient management recommendations, issuing soil health cards every 3 years for all landholdings, to capture fertility changes from plant uptake or other natural causes over time.\n\nSoil Health Management for Sugarcane — Summary:\nSoil health for sugarcane cultivation is managed in India through two complementary channels: (1) technical soil-nutrient replenishment guidance from ICAR-IISR, and (2) the Government of India's Soil Health Card (SHC) Scheme, implemented at the state level (including by the Karnataka Department of Agriculture), which provides farmers with periodic soil testing and nutrient-specific recommendations.\n\nSoil Health Management for Sugarcane — K